  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe propose a fundamentally different approach based on structured optimization and shape-preserving transformations. Instead of treating each point independently, we parameterize the function h(x) as a combination of elementary wavelets/oscillations. This leverages the observation that optimal functions often exhibit regular structural patterns in their correlations. The core idea is to:\n1. Generate candidate functions by combining predefined elementary building blocks\n2. For each candidate, compute the maximum overlap using geometric properties\n3. Use a tree-based search to navigate the solution space efficiently\n4. Hybridize with local optimization to polish promising candidates\n\nThis differs from the original multi-phase numerical optimization by:\n1. Using exact mathematical derivations instead of numerical experiments\n2. Exploiting structural properties of optimal solutions\n3. Potentially finding substantively better constants through geometric optimization\n\nKey benefits:\n- Reduced dimensionality by focusing on parameterized shapes\n- Better interpretability of optimal solutions\n- Potentially faster convergence to meaningful structures\n\nMathematical specification:\nDefine elementary functions f_m(x) on [0,2] for m=1,...,M with parameter sets p_m \u2208 \u211d^{d_m}. The candidate function is:\n\nh(x) = sgn( \u2211_{m=1}^M A_m \u00b7 f_m(x; p_m) )\n\nBut normalized to satisfy \u222bh dx = 1 and h \u2208 {0,1}.\n\nThe optimization variables are:\n- Amplitudes A_m \u2265 0\n- Parameters p_m\n- Activation masks for f_m\n\nConstraints:\n1. Probability constraint: \u222bh dx = 1\n2. Domain: supports \u2286 [0,2]\n\nWe use majorization and contraction to ensure feasibility.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialize set of M elementary wavelets (Gaussian mixture, square pulses, etc.)\n2. Define function to generate candidate by sampling parameters and combining\n3. Compute objective using closed-form geometric properties for candidate functions\n4. Use tournament selection with local mutation for global search\n5. Employ gradient-based polishing for promising candidates\n6. Automatically enforce constraints via probability warping\n7. Maintain population of non-dominated solutions\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Elementary function library: Gaussian mixtures, rectangular pulses, Chebyshev polynomials\n- Global search: Covariance matrix adaptation (CMA-ES)\n- Local polishing: Sequential quadratic programming\n- Parameter space adaptation: Hilbert space-filling curves\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n- Major phase: CMA-ES with success-history based adaptation\n- Minor phase: Local optimization on best candidates\n- Feasibility repair: Use logistic sigmoid to map unconstrained to feasible\n- Convergence detection: Pareto front analysis in objective&quality space\n- Runtime estimation: 60% global search, 30% local, 10% adaptivity\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Verify \u2211h[i] == n/2\n2. Confirm 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n3. Run VerifyObjective(h) implementation that matches task evaluator\n4. For h[i] out of bounds: match feasible projection\n5. For undefined terms: use analytic bounds\n6. Main loop: adaptive termination based on improvement rate\n\nGlobal fallback solution:\nIf constrained fails GenerateCandidate(), use DE initialized with:\n- Population: interpolated samples from initial_h_values\n- Mutation: polynomial interpolation\n- Crossover: harmonic mean crossover\n- Restart: When trapped in local optimum\n\nSpecial consideration for bounds:\n- Use monomials to expand valid region\n- Compute accessible volume heuristically\n- For h[i] near [0,1]: clip operationally but satisfies integrals",
